  {
   "k": "1tbmat9",
   "en": "The ⟦0⟧'s collapse in the chaos of the 1917 revolutions and the ensuing civil war gave rise to the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics in 1922. Born from the Bolshevik victory over the White forces and foreign intervention, the new Soviet state emerged under the leadership of Vladimir Lenin and the Communist Party, committed to building a socialist society and spreading revolutionary ideology abroad. The early years were marked by economic hardship, reconstruction, and the suppression of political dissent.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "11x8482",
   "en": "Russian Empire"
  },
  {
   "k": "18z4h35",
   "en": "Following Lenin’s death in 1924, Joseph Stalin gradually consolidated power, outmaneuvering rivals within the party. By the early 1930s, the ⟦0⟧ had embarked on a program of rapid industrialization and forced collectivization of agriculture, transforming the nation’s economic base at the cost of widespread famine, suffering, and political repression. The Great Purge of 1936–1938 further tightened Stalin’s grip, eliminating perceived enemies within the military, party, and intelligentsia, but leaving the Red Army weakened on the eve of global conflict.",
   "frags": 1
  },
  {
   "k": "h9htf",
   "en": "In foreign affairs, the Soviet Union initially sought collective security against the rise of fascism, engaging in negotiations with the ⟦0⟧ and ⟦1⟧. However, Western reluctance to commit to firm guarantees led Moscow to sign the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact with the ⟦2⟧ in August 1939, a non-aggression treaty that secretly divided Eastern Europe into spheres of influence. Following the pact, the USSR annexed eastern ⟦3⟧, the Baltic states, and Bessarabia, and fought the Winter War against ⟦4⟧, gaining territory but revealing serious deficiencies in its armed forces.",
   "frags": 5
  },
  {
   "k": "vcugn7",
   "en": "United Kingdom"
  },
  {
   "k": "y6f35m",
   "en": "France"
  },
  {
   "k": "i87c38",
   "en": "German Reich"
  },
  {
   "k": "399cmg",
   "en": "This uneasy arrangement with Germany ended abruptly on 22 June 1941, when the Wehrmacht launched Operation Barbarossa, the largest invasion in history. The Soviet Union, caught off guard, suffered devastating early losses but gradually halted the German advance through immense sacrifice and the mobilization of its vast resources. The turning points at Moscow, Stalingrad, and Kursk shifted the momentum, and by 1945 the Red Army had pushed deep into Eastern Europe, capturing Berlin in May. Victorious yet scarred by immense human and material losses, the Soviet Union emerged from the war as a superpower, dominating Eastern Europe and entering into a global rivalry with the ⟦0⟧ that would define the Cold War.",
   "frags": 1
